#{[17592186581747 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}5{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582344 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186582344 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{1}{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582346 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582348 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{17}($\n{{field-1}} $)$"] [17592186582348 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4({e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-16I \\\\ 17I & =-{e}^{-4x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )))-(-\\frac{1}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(-1+0)+\\frac{1}{17}(1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}+\\frac{1}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(1+{e}^{-4\\pi }).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582360 :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1).$ Then, since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(xy+yz+1)}^{3}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1)$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$    \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)i+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot (x+z))j+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+z}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1+1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.$ \n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=3.$"] [17592186582361 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}4x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186582366 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(2-2x+9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,2].$"] [17592186582369 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /4)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.$"] [17592186582378 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186582385 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582389 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$"] [17592186582390 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y=|x|-1$ and the line $y=2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,3]$"] [17592186582396 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-4$ and $y=-x+2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,5]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-5,5]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-6,6]$"] [17592186582397 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186582397 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-J \\\\ I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}\\right){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-\\pi }-{e}^{\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582404 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the value of the double integral ${∬}_{R}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{ }{e}^{2x-y}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[0,1]\\times [2,5]?$"] [17592186582407 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}(5{x}^{4}+9{y}^{2})\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,1].$"] [17592186582409 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186582409 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{e}^{5x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{5x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{5x}}{5}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{5x}}{5}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{e}^{5x}}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{5x}}{5}-\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582413 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<2,\\,1<y<e, \\\\ xy,\\, & \\text{if}\\,2<x<4,\\,1<y<e,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186583003 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $u(t)=\\sqrt{3t+7}$ is continuous at $t=-1$ and \n\n$$\nu(-1)=\\sqrt{3(-1)+7}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=2$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=-1,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n${lim}_{t\\to -1}f(u(t))=f(u(-1))=f(2).$"] [17592186583008 :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the adjacent $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ hypotenuse to the given angle $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({36}^{∘}) & =\\frac{7.28}{AC} \\\\ AC\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({36}^{∘}) & =7.28 \\\\ AC & =\\frac{7.28}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({36}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{7.28}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({36}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.28}{(0.809\\,01…)} \\\\  & =8.998\\,57… \\\\  & ≈9.0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186583010 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{20\\pi }{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{20\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20\\pi }{3}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{20\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{20\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{20\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$"] [17592186583014 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $u(t)={t}^{2}+1$ is continuous at $t=2$ and \n\n$$\nu(2)={2}^{2}+1=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=5$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=2,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(u(t))=f(u(2))=f(5).$"] [17592186583020 :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{6\\pi }{2}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n3\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(3\\pi )}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ units down. Hence, $D=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+(-2)=-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)-2.\n$$"] [17592186583022 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(2x+1)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583029 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186583030 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{6}{5{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.$"] [17592186583034 :question/problem "${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{z}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}z=$"] [17592186583040 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{54x}{({x}^{2}+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583047 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.$"] [17592186583055 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nI. The function has asymptotes at $x=\\pi n.$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$\n\nIII. The function is strictly increasing on the interval $(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186583055 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is strictly increasing on each interval                    $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ).$ If we take $n=-1$ we get the interval $(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186583069 :question/problem "$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,${{field-1}} $+\\text{ }C$"] [17592186583074 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583077 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{{({y}^{3}-2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.$"] [17592186583083 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{7}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)}={630}^{∘}.$"] [17592186583086 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n3{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=3{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}=6.\n$$"] [17592186583584 :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}?$"] [17592186583590 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$\n\n![](images/ae/ae746b3869a36bef6740e75f1bd6c82ebf296d3a65de5860cd9dc43fc09f3c38.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $\\pi$ from the right, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,{\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186583591 :question/problem "Which value of $w$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2w-3}{3w}=\\frac{1-w}{2w}-\\frac{4w-1}{9w}?$"] [17592186583611 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{9}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={320}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{16\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${320}^{∘},$ which lies in the $4\\text{th}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/e5/e5d543864e66475bb2e02a6a7188ca497be0d159c700141ea3a105e9bb1615fd.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{9}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{16\\pi }{9}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{16\\pi }{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{9}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{9}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{9}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{9}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{9}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186583616 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{9}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={160}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{8\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${160}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/8a/8a144054987696db35b79baca0ac60761e5549b32dcceca0ec44ef9f46b142ec.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{9}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{8\\pi }{9}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{8\\pi }{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{9}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{9}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{9}\\right)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186583618 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=4,$ and the curve $y={x}^{2}.$ What is ${∬}_{D}(y+x){e}^{x+y}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186583621 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c93a3a1d720e45d551ba3225b20250d512f219d123d5f9376094daec43c981a.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=-2x,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$"] [17592186583624 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the line $y=-2x$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{3-x},$ above the line $y=0.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186583628 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0d6ec515c60c3bdda815f57005ece7d4de7de0a8251058542a0627da4366083.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the line $y=3$ enclosed by the lines $y=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $x=6.$"] [17592186583630 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=(x+2)^{2}$ and $y=2x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186583639 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ What is ${∬}_{D}(x-{y}^{2})\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186583641 :question/problem "![](images/d5/d594f3356668e3bc605e6655ec92787d9e130be12f3e9e01b9cc482c6897070a.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{3},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$"] [17592186583650 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x(1-y)\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\}.$"] [17592186583650 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x(1-y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}x(1-y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}x(1-y)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}(1-y)[{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}(1-y){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{y}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}(1-y)(\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}(1-y)(1-2{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}(1-y-2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}(1-y-2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}+\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186583651 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.$"] [17592186583651 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}2y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{y}^{2}]_{x}^{1-x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}((1-x)^{2}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}(1-2x)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2x]_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186584334 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{3}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186584339 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186584349 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. $2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. $2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2z}}{t{e}^{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. $2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2z-1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [17592186584354 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{2}^{3x/2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186584361 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x\\cdot {7}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186584369 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z}}{t{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [17592186584371 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{3}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186584372 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [17592186584395 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186584406 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z}}{t{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$"] [17592186585031 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/14/143519dfc291a32f5c617b70a4297643429b18eea64b7ad4736758c60c3a7e27.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{48}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}=4\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [17592186585036 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{10x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x<0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x &={[\\frac{k}{10}{e}^{10x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{10}=1\\,⟹\\,k=10.\n$$"] [17592186585038 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186585038 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{0}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186585060 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186585060 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x+2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x+2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186585067 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186585069 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186585069 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{6\\pi }{3}+6n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne 2\\pi +6n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne 2\\pi +6n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [17592186585082 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x},\\, & x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 1$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186585082 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 1,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}-0 \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186585090 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. ${⋃}_{i=0}^{194}\\,[\\,i\\,]_{195}=Z$\n\n2\\. $[\\,5\\,]_{195}\\cap [\\,127\\,]_{195}=∅$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{195}=\\{[\\,i\\,]_{195}\\,∣\\,0\\le i<195\\}$"] [17592186585093 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/e1/e169b457c3216ffb18463b3e37a345b708e2d5efb2af1076b25f397867d7a826.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+7=7-\\sqrt{2}.$"] [17592186585109 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}$ on the interval $x\\le 2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x &=\\frac{k}{2(4-x)^{2}}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{2}=\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{8}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$"] [17592186585714 :question/problem "Given that $A=2$ and $B=4,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$"] [17592186585749 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186585749 :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$"] [17592186585772 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$"] [17592186585793 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees?. Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186585793 :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={360}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x={360}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."] [17592186585794 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{360}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$-{360}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={0}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}).$\n\nThe angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=0.$"] [17592186586611 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(kx-3)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ky-3)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ky-3)+1 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ky-3) & =x-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(ky-3) & =\\frac{x-1}{4} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ky-3)} & ={e}^{(x-1)/4} \\\\ ky-3 & ={e}^{(x-1)/4} \\\\ ky & ={e}^{(x-1)/4}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}({e}^{(x-1)/4}+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {e}^{(x-1)/4}+\\frac{3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {e}^{(x-1)/4}+\\frac{3}{k}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=3.$"] [17592186587299 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}||f(s)||$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1},\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2},\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1}⟩.$"] [17592186587325 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers except at the integer multiples of $\\pi$: $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n\n- The zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points that are not in the domain: $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ and at each asymptote, the graph tends to $-\\infty$ from the left and $\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d0510d5dc22d3ecb81f4cbaa67789c84b1fdc1a40065c0d2527683271e261a6.png)"] [17592186587363 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+6=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+6=7.$"] [17592186588194 :question/problem "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$ for which the polar curve $r=5$ has vertical tangents, given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186588211 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{5}⁡(8{x}^{3})=3.$"] [17592186588231 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}(x+\\frac{2C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{2C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}(x+\\frac{2C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/64/6438f318c33223abe9f3d2102e6f9f35bf2a8a6e887955ec9230d22e8294536a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{2C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$"] [17592186588247 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡(16{x}^{4})-{log}_{8}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)=1.$"] [17592186588256 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,g(2)=6,\\,{g}^{′}(2)=3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=2.$"] [17592186589078 :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{kx+2}-2$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the value of $k?$"] [17592186589089 :question/problem "A stick of length $2$ is broken at a point chosen uniformly at random along its length. Let the continuous random variable $X$ denote the distance from the left end of the stick to the break point, and let $Y=Y(X)$ denote the length of the longest piece.\n\nExpressing the integrands in the following equation as polynomials, we have\n\n$\\text{E}[Y]={\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{d}x\\,\\,+\\,\\,{\\int }_{1}^{2}$\n{{field-2}} $\\text{d}x$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is  \n{{field-3}}."] [17592186589127 :question/problem "A point is chosen uniformly at random from the interval $[0,1].$ Let the continuous random variable $X$ be the distance from the origin to the chosen point. Consider the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),(X,0),$ and $(X,2X).$ Let $A=A(X)$ denote the area of this triangle.\n\nExpressing the integrand in the following equation as a polynomial, we have \n\n$E[A]={\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n{{field-1}} $dx$\n\nTherefore, the expected area of the triangle is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186589974 :question/worked-solution "![](images/3c/3ca3fa1fe2759b972b9056c8a0b6c0f9a50058517a169e24c9a1999005719b2d.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the balloon's height, and $z$ is the distance between the child and the balloon. The quantity $z=120$ centimeters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{120} \\\\ y & =120\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(120\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(120\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(120\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{60} & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{60}$ radians per second."] [17592186589978 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y+1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y+1] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }(y+1){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }(y+1)[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{32}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186589982 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y\\}.$\n\n![](images/2c/2c984b3c51548a6c9181575cefe27427d12f07c499c4b44fd3fd0bddb0d710af.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}cy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}cy\\,dx]dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[cxy]_{x=y/2}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}(cy(2-y)-c\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}(2cy-\\frac{3c{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}(2cy-\\frac{3c{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y & ={[c{y}^{2}-\\frac{c{y}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16c}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{16c}{27}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$"] [17592186589988 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$\n\n![](images/f6/f60587953ec9bbe05689382b641677058ce8ba070917db340a0ba793170d0951.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}c{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}c{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{c{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9c{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9c{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =[3c{x}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n3c=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186589994 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le {y}^{3}\\le 8\\}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ecf0160f62333198c74110695da7f1800836f20a5d646e436df2520ddd1acfe6.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{x=1}^{x={y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(cy-\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(cy-\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{c}{y}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2c+\\frac{c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{2}+c) \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"] [17592186590007 :question/problem "Evaluate the following Integral.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-3}}\n$))$"] [17592186590008 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}16{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186590017 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /10}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{3}\\right)+2)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186590018 :question/problem "Let $D=[1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{8},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2y}{8},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$"] [17592186590018 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nf(2,2)=2-2\\cdot 2=-2≱0.\\,\\times\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy}{8}]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y+3}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+3y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2xy]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}(2y+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[{y}^{2}+\\frac{3y}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\""] [17592186590020 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{5}{1-{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x$  for $|x|<1.$"] [17592186590024 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $h(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xy)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xy)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xy| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xy<1.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${h}_{y}$ is the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}} & ={D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186590060 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3/2}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{4x}{3}\\right)+7x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186590073 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }8{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186590762 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-3|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186590765 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186591653 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x\\sqrt{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186591666 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=(1-x)^{2}$ with $x\\le 1,$ and $y=\\sqrt{2-x},$ from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186591667 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [17592186591676 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{35}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186591703 :question/problem "Given that $K=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(K).$"] [17592186591707 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2-3z}\\,\\text{d}z=$"] [17592186592241 :question/problem "If\n    $U=\\begin{bmatrix}-v & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=4$, what is the value of $v?$"] [17592186592242 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186592243 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$\nthen\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$AB=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"5px\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186592244 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [17592186592245 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2-x$ and $y=4-{x}^{2},$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$"] [17592186592254 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186592255 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line $x-2=y=\\frac{z}{-1}.$"] [17592186592261 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nfind the minor ${M}_{12}$ of the entry ${p}_{12}.$\n\n${M}_{12}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186592263 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,dA,\\,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{3}{x}\\}.$"] [17592186592263 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{3}{x}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/x}^{3/x}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/x}^{3/x}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{1/x}^{3/x}\\frac{2}{1+(2xy)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2xy)]}_{1/x}^{3/x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2))\\,dx \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2))[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2))\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2))\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186592264 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$Y=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{13}$ of the entry ${y}_{13}.$\n\n${M}_{13}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186592268 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n+1}.$"] [17592186592278 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z & =2\\,⟹\\,z=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}+({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+4+(y-5)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x+4+(y-5)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+2+(y-5)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x+1)+(y-5)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 2(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6}=1.$"] [17592186592280 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${u}_{21}$ in the matrix\n$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & j & k-2j \\\\ kj & j & k \\\\ 7 & -k & j\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186592289 :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{n-1}}{{12}^{n}}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }5{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{n}$"] [17592186592289 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{n-1}}{{12}^{n}}$ starts with $n=3.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{4-1}}{{12}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{3-1}}{{12}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{12}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{12}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{12}^{4}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{12}^{3}}{{6}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{6}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }5{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{5{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}}{5{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge."] [17592186592292 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186592300 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\,dy\\,dx=$"] [17592186592302 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$"] [17592186593223 :question/problem "${cos}^{2}⁡x(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1)=$"] [17592186593229 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the sphere, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{\\left(\\frac{A}{4\\pi }\\right)}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =16\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(16\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =256\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =256\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =64 \\\\ r & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186593258 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+13x+8}{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186593261 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186593278 :question/problem "If\n$U=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$,\nthen find the transpose of $U.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\" rowspan=\"2\">\n${U}^{T}=$ \n    </td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n      {{field-1}}\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n      {{field-2}}\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n      {{field-3}}\n    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      {{field-4}}\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      {{field-5}}\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      {{field-6}}\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186593291 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+16}{(x-7)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594142 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3S}-\\frac{1}{S+3}=\\frac{2}{3{S}^{2}+9S}.$\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594154 :question/problem "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}} &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ is true for all {{field-9}}."] [17592186594154 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}} &=\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{1}}) &=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ is true for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186594158 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{n+1}-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=0}^{0}{3}^{i} &=\\frac{1}{2}({{field-1}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({{field-3}}).\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({{field-4}}).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{n+1}-1)$ for all $n\\ge 0.$"] [17592186594158 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{n+1}-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(0).$ To do this, we substitute $n=0$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=0}^{0}{3}^{i} &=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{0+1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 0.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{k+1}-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=0,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{k+2}-1).\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{n+1}-1)$ for all $n\\ge 0.$"] [17592186594159 :question/problem "If $S(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(5i+3),$ then $S(k+1)=$"] [17592186594160 :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*"] [17592186594165 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$"] [17592186594174 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594175 :question/problem "If $S(k)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}i(i-1)(i+1),$ then $S(k+1)=$"] [17592186594177 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-21}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594181 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}n(3n+1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(3i-1) &=\\frac{1}{2}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}n(3n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186594181 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}n(3n+1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(3i-1) &=\\frac{1}{2}(1)(3+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}k(3k+1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(3i-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}(k+1)(3(k+1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(k+1)(3k+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}n(3n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186594186 :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+3.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ determine $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594190 :question/problem "$\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3z}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{2}}{4{y}^{2}}=$"] [17592186594215 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-3}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,\\,21\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [17592186594227 :question/problem "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}} &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ is true for all {{field-9}}."] [17592186594227 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}} &=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1}} &=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ is true for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186594236 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{n}^{2}(n+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{i}^{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{n}^{2}(n+1)^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186594236 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{n}^{2}(n+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{i}^{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{2}(1+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{k}^{2}(k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}(k+1)^{2}(k+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{i}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{n}^{2}(n+1)^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186594668 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186594669 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{(6x-2)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594672 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186594683 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t.$"] [17592186594687 :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because **addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nin $Z.$ Then,\n\n$$\ndet(A+B)=det(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHence, $A+B\\notin Z.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in $Z$ since $det(-A)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot det(A)=1\\cdot 0=0,$ and $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ multiplication by a scalar $\\alpha \\in R$ is closed since $det(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.$ Hence, $\\alpha A\\in Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Multiplication of matrices with real entries by a scalar is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$"] [17592186594690 :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186594691 :question/problem "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$1+\\sqrt{5}+\\frac{5}{2!}+\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3!}+⋯+\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{n}}{n!}+⋯=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594694 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-{(2x+4)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594705 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-4t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,${{field-1}}\n$+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186594708 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594709 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-5}^{-3}\\frac{60}{|x+5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186594712 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{36|x|}{{(2+3{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594714 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [-4,4],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R \\\\ |{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$"] [17592186594718 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186594719 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594725 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=3-|x|.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [1,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,3]$"] [17592186595925 :question/problem "The heights, in centimeters, of six randomly selected tulips drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n35,\\,37,\\,42,\\,51,\\,48,\\,51\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({X}_{i}-44)^{2}}{n-1}$ for this particular sample."] [17592186595933 :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{30}$ for $y\\in [0,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186595953 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}(n{X}_{i}+\\sigma )^{2}$\n\nII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{10}+{X}_{20}}{\\sigma }$\n\nIII. $\\frac{\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20})}{n}$"] [17592186595978 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu$ is known and $\\sigma$ is unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${X}_{1}+{X}_{20}-\\mu$\n\nII. ${X}_{2}+{X}_{19}-\\sigma$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\mu }{{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$"] [17592186595979 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve  $r(t)=(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$"] [17592186595988 :question/problem "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n2.5,\\,3.1,\\,3.6,\\,2.8,\\,2.1,\\,3.5\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}$ for this particular sample."] [17592186596005 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186596625 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=${{field-1}}."] [17592186596625 :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,f(x)=f\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,{f}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\frac{4}{5}.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})=0+{8}^{1}=8, \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))=\\frac{4}{5}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡0=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596646 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=\\frac{7}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7}{2}-1=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-y+z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2+(-2) \\\\  & =3-2-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-3y-z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2-(-2) \\\\  & =2-6+2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ |  | $-2$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-5)=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)≈-7.$"] [17592186596649 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}({f}^{′}(x)-\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}"] [17592186596672 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}"] [17592186596673 :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{-8}^{-5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi -3,$ then ${\\int }_{-8}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}."] [17592186596681 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa57af0c1950be0b52f9a4c5d9abe8dd86fc82ef874f09beed6cbb1e924f31b.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/32/3296a5305332592542386ac439b911fec19a6ecfb2d82bf5aec440177c30d47f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/47/475a2dc2e5e9301afeb1e9d123568912a98469514c4385f765ecb41f29c8c430.png)"] [17592186596682 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}sin\\,({x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186596684 :question/problem "Given that $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186596684 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot [{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)] \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596690 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3b4e48221f7896f6082773552ef009b14ab0cbaf626068040fa4c1b87a71a45.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/30/307aee5d0083ed352e9f24acb5ed1107f6096b8805810190841317e77a891627.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/96/96130e419134ae5825f4ec68987e4c2d54e1ee3521c5cfd2a5f7d1e29bfb0954.png)"] [17592186596691 :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi$ intersects the line $x-y=0.$"] [17592186596695 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/f9/f9d1127a2bdde6e2b9910a4404a73b9e8b994ff8400e8607af8b6931013f2533.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f4/f4abb32b9a492e7d39b4504af7cf2a180e74dcca679804ed8198089cb68b7fb6.png)"] [17592186596701 :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined as $g(x)={\\int }_{-5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+12x+35}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186596702 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}3{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x$"] [17592186596710 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [17592186596714 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=-1,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=-1,$ $z=1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+3y-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)-0 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1-y+2z \\\\  & =1-(-1)+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =z-x \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |  | $1$ | $4$ |  | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $-2$ |  |  | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-2,$\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈\\frac{7}{3},$\nand\n$w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186597539 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$"] [17592186597539 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\})=3.$"] [17592186597544 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [17592186597551 :question/problem "Write the following series in sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$5-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{5}{9}+…={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}"] [17592186597555 :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.$ What is the first component of $f?$"] [17592186597557 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x}{1-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186597558 :question/problem "Consider the sequence starting from ${a}_{1}=2$ with the property $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{5}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$ Write the sum to infinity $S$ of the terms of this sequence using sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}"] [17592186597563 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 9{x}^{8}(9+2{x}^{9})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186597570 :question/problem "Consider the sequence starting from ${a}_{1}=9$  with the property $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$ Write the sum to infinity $S$ of the terms of this series using sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}"] [17592186597575 :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$"] [17592186597578 :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the second component of $f?$"] [17592186597579 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [17592186597588 :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{49}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$"] [17592186597589 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{cx{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{cx}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{cx}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{4}=1\\,⇒\\,c=4.\n$$"] [17592186597591 :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{36}=1.$ What is the first component of $f?$"] [17592186597595 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$, \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ."] [17592186597604 :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the second component of $f?$"] [17592186597616 :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $f?$"] [17592186597702 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{2}{y}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{m}\\right)^{4}=$"] [17592186597704 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{f}{3}\\right)^{3}\\left(\\frac{5}{v}\\right)^{4}=$"] [17592186597714 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{q}{2}\\right)^{4}\\left(\\frac{5}{a}\\right)^{2}=$"] [17592186597724 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\frac{4d}{2}\\right)^{4}=$"] [17592186597732 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{5}{p}\\right)^{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{p}\\right)^{3}=$"] [17592186598568 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$ \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186598573 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186598575 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598575 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already know that the statement is true for some value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{k+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {4}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598577 :question/problem "${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,${{field-1}}"] [17592186598584 :question/problem "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [17592186598589 :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186598595 :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [17592186598595 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{1} & {2}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{k} & {2}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{k+1} & {2}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{k} & {2}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({2}^{k}) & {2}^{k}+({2}^{k}-1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1} & {2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{k+1} & {2}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{k+1}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186598597 :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186598602 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"] [17592186598604 :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186598608 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{16}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{16}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3$, we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-16,$ $b=5,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a-b}{c}=\\frac{-16-5}{3}=-7.$"] [17592186598609 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186598618 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598618 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598621 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598621 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2k & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598622 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\beg