gebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598625 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186598626 :question/problem "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [17592186598627 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{n}-1 & {2}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{n}-1 & {2}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N$\n\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598627 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{n}-1 & {2}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{1}-1 & {2}^{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{k}-1 & {2}^{k}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{k+1}-1 & {2}^{k+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {2}^{n}-1 & {2}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186598629 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{5+1}{-2}=-3.$"] [17592186598632 :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [17592186598632 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2k & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2k & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2k+2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186599194 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{(\\pi /3)^{3}}{3!}-\\frac{(\\pi /3)^{5}}{5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-\\pi /3)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$"] [17592186599211 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{27}{8\\cdot 3!}-\\frac{243}{32\\cdot 5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-3)^{2n+1}}{{2}^{2n+1}(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$"] [17592186599214 :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=-3$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3.$"] [17592186599219 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186599219 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-1+0-4-2=-7\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=2+0-2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=-18+18=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"."] [17592186599221 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $1-\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(5\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186599226 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{(2\\pi /3)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(2\\pi /3)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(2\\pi /3)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$"] [17592186599234 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186599234 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$\n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=1+1=2\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0=0\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the sets are orthonormal.\""] [17592186599240 :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{21}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{12}=2$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$"] [17592186599243 :question/problem "$1-\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(4\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(4\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=$"] [17592186599253 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and second equations, we get ${x}_{3}=-{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=0$, respectively. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+0+(-{x}_{4})+{x}_{4} &=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$"] [17592186599266 :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{21}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{12}=-2$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2.$"] [17592186599795 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=0,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=0,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2-3y+z \\\\  & =2-3\\cdot 0+0 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+2y \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =y-2w \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $0$ | $0$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+0=0 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{1}{2},$\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈0,$\nand\n$w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186599799 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3-x, & x<1 \\\\ 2x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186599811 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-1}.$"] [17592186599812 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)=4\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$"] [17592186599815 :question/worked-solution "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4-y-2z \\\\  & =4-1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+w \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =y+z \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+0=1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{2},$\n$z\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1,$\nand\n$w\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.$"] [17592186599820 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a6077b85e5dd3bc54daa6eaf4c6205c631443f7a6825b5313f7f62e2776af2f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186599826 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2\\sqrt{x}-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186599828 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3z-1)\\,\\text{d}z=$"] [17592186599829 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{2y}-y)\\text{d}y=$"] [17592186599830 :question/problem "![](images/65/652109189703c545f1d79a31a2f9d6b14d94790b4e5fb2b0888b279b197d0cdb.png)\n\nConsider the graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186599831 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186599833 :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186600345 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<0.$"] [17592186600360 :question/problem "Given that\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & x\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand $det(D)=481,$ find the value of $x.$"] [17592186600371 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2}+x)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186600378 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(2\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +2\\beta &=\\frac{3}{50}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 2\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{9}{650},\\beta =\\frac{3}{325}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{9}{650}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{325}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{9}{650}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{325}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-9,$ $Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-9+3=-6.\n$$"] [17592186600382 :question/problem "Calculate ${U}^{-1}$, where \n$U=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186600385 :question/problem "If $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{{x}^{3}+2}\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186600786 :question/problem "From **left to right**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{48}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{16}=1$ interceps the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$"] [17592186600789 :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{18}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,-4+\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$"] [17592186600797 :question/problem "$\\int (g(x)+\\sqrt{h(x)})\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186600798 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. \n\nIn part 2 we found that $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}+f\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+(3+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =(2-3(0))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+(3+2(0))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"] [17592186600804 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\right).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\" rowspan=\"2\">\n  $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\right)=\\,-$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$3$\n    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">\n  {{field-1}} \n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186600808 :question/problem "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{100}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$"] [17592186600812 :question/problem "$\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186600818 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $det(A)=k-12.$ Find the value of $k.$"] [17592186600821 :question/problem "From **left to right**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,$ and $\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$"] [17592186600825 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)+1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+5)+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)+1) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+5) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+5) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+5)}{3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+5)}{3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1+2)+5)}{3-ln\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3-ln\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & ≈1.93.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.93.$"] [17592186600830 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0,\\,x\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=$"] [17592186601452 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}={[\\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{1}{2}]}^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$"] [17592186601452 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [17592186601454 :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=6$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601463 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+10}$ between $x=-5$ and $x=1$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{6}{n}$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601464 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in the case when $k=16.$\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601466 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{2}{(0.6)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601466 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{(0.6)}^{1}=2.7.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{9}{2}{(0.6)}^{2}}{\\frac{9}{2}{(0.6)}^{1}}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{2}{(0.6)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.7}{1-0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.7}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601469 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.$"] [17592186601469 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4t\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{4{t}^{2}}{2}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)}^{2}(2{t}^{2})-\\int (2{t}^{2})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}-4\\int t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\text{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=t\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int t\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t))\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}-4(\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={(2{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{2})|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2{e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+{e}^{2})-(2{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+(1)^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601474 :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-7$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{3}{4}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601481 :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.$"] [17592186601484 :question/problem "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$"] [17592186601486 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }5{\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{\\,n-3}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601493 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }96(0.2)^{2n}.$"] [17592186601495 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s.$"] [17592186601495 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={s}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=2s \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}s}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}s}\\,\\text{d}s=\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}s}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =({s}^{2})(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s))-\\int -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=s & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}s}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}s}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)-\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+4\\int s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+4(\\frac{1}{2}s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=4{C}_{1}.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)+2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-2{\\pi }^{2}+1-1 \\\\  & =-2{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601496 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(0.4)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601496 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}=0.2.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(0.4)}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(0.4)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{1-0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601499 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-1)=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-1)=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(0) \\\\ 0 & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (0))\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\pi )\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [17592186601500 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 3{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186601500 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}{x}^{3}-\\int {x}^{3}\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-4\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}))\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-4(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{9}-4D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$"] [17592186601501 :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=15$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{4}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$"] [17592186601504 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-3y-1 \\\\ xy-3x+y+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-3y-1)=2x+2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-3y-1)=2y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-3x+y+1)=y-3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-3x+y+1)=x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+2 & 2y-3 \\\\ y-3 & x+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+2 & 2(1)-3 \\\\ 1-3 & 1+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186601506 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{2e}54{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(${{field-1}}$)$"] [17592186601506 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=54{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 54{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=18{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 54{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))}^{2}(18{x}^{3})-\\int (18{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18{x}^{3}{({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))}^{2}-\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x/2)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 54{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =18{x}^{3}{({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))}^{2}-\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =18{x}^{3}{({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))}^{2}-\\frac{12{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x/2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{18{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡(x/2)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{12{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/2)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18{ln}^{2}⁡(x/2)-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/2)+4)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{36D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation, we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡(x/2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{2e}54{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡(x/2))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18{ln}^{2}⁡(x/2)-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/2)+4)]}_{2}^{2e} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2e)^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18{ln}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{2e}{2}\\right)-12ln\\,\\left(\\frac{2e}{2}\\right)+4)-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18{ln}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right)-12ln\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18{ln}^{2}⁡e-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+4)-\\frac{8}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18{ln}^{2}⁡1-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(18-12+4)-\\frac{8}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{80{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{32}{{ln}^{2}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(\\,80{e}^{3}-32\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601507 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy-{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-xy\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{x}^{2})=y-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{x}^{2})=x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-xy)=-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-xy)=2y-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}y-2x & x \\\\ -y & 2y-x\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1-2(1) & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2(1)-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [17592186601514 :question/problem "Express $z=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [17592186601514 :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince\n $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and\n    $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601517 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601517 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{2}}{3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{1}}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601518 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}⁡(2x-3)-{log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)={log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4x+11).$"] [17592186601521 :question/problem "Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x-3)(x-5)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x-3)(x-5)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601522 :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601894 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186601906 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }/2}\\frac{x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186601918 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-3t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,${{field-1}}\n$+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186601926 :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186601928 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{e/3}^{{e}^{2}/3}\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601933 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4-5y} & =21 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4-5y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n4-5y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-5y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21) \\\\ -5y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21)-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21)-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(21).$"] [17592186601938 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=1.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=1\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186601948 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186602398 :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -3 & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186602404 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+16y\\} & =L\\{10{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(4(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{10{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(4(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))$, so $f(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$ The transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}=10\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\frac{40}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\nL\\{10{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(4(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\}={e}^{-\\pi s/2}\\cdot \\frac{40}{{s}^{2}+16}=\\frac{40{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+16Y(s) & =\\frac{40{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+16)Y(s) & =\\frac{40{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{40{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186602405 :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}D$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${I}_{3}\\cdot D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |"] [17592186602414 :question/problem "Let\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThen, the dimensions of $AB$ are{{field-1}}$\\times$\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186602416 :question/problem "Find $Pt$ if \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$t=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186602418 :question/problem "Find the product $CD$, if\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & \\sqrt{2} & 10 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -7 & \\pi  & 0 \\\\ 6 & -\\sqrt{11} & -\\frac{9}{4} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 12 & -3 & \\sqrt{13} & -e\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $CD=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [17592186602419 :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186602424 :question/problem "If\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthen $AB=$"] [17592186602424 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}={AB}_{1\\times 3}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,9=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,2=2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-7=2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186602432 :question/problem "Find $ST$, if \n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186602437 :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n${f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}={lim}_{x\\to 1}(-1\\,-$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{x}^{2}).$"] [17592186602439 :question/problem "Find $Cy$ if\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 9 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Cy=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186602441 :question/problem "The exact value of the area $A$ from part 7 can be expressed using definite integral notation as\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186602444 :question/problem "Determine $AB$ given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"1\">$AB=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186602449 :question/problem "![](images/ba/bae5904772e7ba190a516cbe66839f0d2e38775dfdcad836314d5945bfd215ad.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$"] [17592186602449 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/1b/1b22d5486ce7cd4ed083842ba019b454ff711f9fc5b50718b97e58e95ce647ad.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/4f/4f2ae4819d2534d2e6abfbfe4a2842597f1ad0845f7925f5f74c359b69f7ba1f.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186602452 :question/problem "Write the expression for the Riemann sum from part 7 in sigma notation.\n\nThe right Riemann sum $\\,=\\,0.5\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(-0.125{i}^{3}+$\n{{field-1}} ${i}^{2}-1.5i+3)$"] [17592186602457 :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(-3{x}^{2}-$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{h}^{2}).$"] [17592186602458 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [17592186603006 :question/problem "Write down an expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral, and express the integral as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n$A={\\int }_{2}^{5}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186603011 :question/problem "Given that \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{a}_{i}=8,$\nfind the value of \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}4.5{a}_{i}.$"] [17592186603013 :question/problem "Given that \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{43}{f}_{i}=\\frac{5}{2}$\nand\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{43}{g}_{i}=-3,$\nfind the value of \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{43}({f}_{i}+{g}_{i}).$"] [17592186603023 :question/problem "Given that \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{13}{s}_{i}=7$\nand\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{13}{t}_{i}=2,$\nfind the value of \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{13}(2{s}_{i}+9{t}_{i}).$"] [17592186603028 :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{52}1.$"] [17592186603038 :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{13}(-3).$"] [17592186603049 :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1ffbd77d1d087ab0e949ad24b47c820a875a51d69409fa34684e4b0718ed1bc.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}."] [17592186603049 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9ec225c7a80c14153a6cbf339c0a8ee9ff3cd2da88205b5b705506616aed7302.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b950099f2224469ebee05b7bb86486e0e2f0786eb2b681cb62434f04c6b7a9d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$"] [17592186603056 :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186603059 :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ebd95908234b7660cb62427cfb3edc0c07d1f96d1fbc643dca7753cc6a1c6a4.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercept of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{16}{5}(x+y)+\\frac{64}{25}=0,$ shown above."] [17592186603073 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{3}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5d/5de26ee67b318de236f4012fefb67088f67e83a3bce50986ce27fe673bf0db45.png)"] [17592186603085 :question/problem "Given that \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{a}_{i}=175,$\nfind the value of \n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}6{a}_{i}.$"] [17592186603173 :question/problem "Express $\\log_{7}\\left(4\\right)+\\log_{7}\\left(6\\right)+\\log_{7}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ as a single logarithm."] [17592186603181 :question/problem "Express $\\log_{8}\\left(4\\right)+\\log_{8}\\left(3\\right)+\\log_{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ as a single logarithm."] [17592186603189 :question/problem "Express $\\log_{4}\\left(4\\right)+\\log_{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\log_{4}\\left(8\\right)$ as a single logarithm."] [17592186604009 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{({e}^{2}-1)/2}{log}_{4}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186604018 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186604037 :question/problem "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186604038 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186604042 :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{2}{7}\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186604047 :question/problem "What is the linear transformation $T$ whose standard matrix is \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186604063 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{8}<Y<\\frac{1}{6}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).$"] [17592186604084 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186604568 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186604626 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$"] [17592186605088 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{38}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{38}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{38}}{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{38}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{38}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{38}} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}: &=\\sqrt{38}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=7{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(7{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-8{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{114}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{114}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{114}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{114}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{38}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{38}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{38}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{114}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{114}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{114}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{114}}|+|\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{114}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{114}}|=\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{114}}.$"] [17592186605089 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{7}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186605094 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}5\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186605111 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605119 :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the second component of $f?$"] [17592186605135 :question/problem "$\\int 2x-3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186605143 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{5}^{4-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186605146 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186605146 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$\n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=1+1=2\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."] [17592186605837 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186605843 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$"] [17592186605843 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=3.$"] [17592186605847 :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /18}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /9}}2x{sec}^{2}⁡(3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186605859 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}=$"] [17592186605890 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡z{csc}^{2}⁡z}{5+{cot}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.$"] [17592186605915 :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\frac{7}{6}$ from $-\\frac{5}{6}$, we first  combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{6}-\\left(-\\frac{7}{6}\\right) &=-\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a common denominator of $6$, so we combine the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{7}{6} &=\\frac{-5+7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $-5+7$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/c4/c47c31bb98aba3dbf76cc8e3e719dfd064ed604caa6ba0b792745b3f905cf7fe.png)\n\nHence, \n$\\frac{-5+7}{6}=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186606798 :question/problem "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"]}
